Stop recompiling a method that does not fit into compiler memory.

A compilation that ran out of memory only doubled the code buffer for the
next attempt. When the compiler's temporary data was what did not fit, the
larger code buffer left even less room for it, and the attempt failed the
same way after doing the same work. A hot method was compiled again every
time a timer tick found it running.

The retry now reserves the largest temporary area, a quarter of the compiler
area. When the retry runs out of memory too, the method is marked impossible
to compile, as the reservation comment already describes. Only the root
compilation records the failure, and an aborted compilation does not count
as running out of memory: an inlined method that aborts it permanently is
marked itself, and its caller compiles again without inlining it.

--- a/cldc/src/vm/share/compiler/Compiler.cpp
+++ b/cldc/src/vm/share/compiler/Compiler.cpp
@@ -372,10 +372,11 @@
     temp_data_size += sizeof *_compiler_state;
 #endif
     const size_t max_temp_data = HeapCapacity * CompilerAreaPercentage/100/4;
-    if( temp_data_size > max_temp_data ) {
+    if( temp_data_size > max_temp_data || method->is_double_size() ) {
       // Don't use more than a quarter of the compiler area to compile any
       // method. This means very big methods would fail to compile and
-      // would be marked as impossible to compile.
+      // would be marked as impossible to compile. A method whose previous
+      // compilation ran out of memory reserves the whole quarter.
       temp_data_size = max_temp_data;
     }
     const int needed = compiled_size + temp_data_size;
@@ -565,7 +566,19 @@
 }

 inline void Compiler::handle_out_of_memory( void ) {
-  method()->set_double_size();
+  if( is_inlining() ) {
+    // The root compilation fails as well and handles the failure.
+    return;
+  }
+  if( method()->is_double_size() ) {
+    // An earlier compilation of this method ran out of memory, so this one
+    // reserved a doubled code buffer and the largest temporary area, and it
+    // ran out as well. Further attempts would fail the same way after the
+    // same work, so interpret the method from now on.
+    set_impossible_to_compile( method(), "out of memory" );
+  } else {
+    method()->set_double_size();
+  }
 }

 #if  ENABLE_INTERNAL_CODE_OPTIMIZER
@@ -906,6 +919,10 @@
